# Contract template for a NEW opponent. Copy, rename, fill in from THEIR answers.
#
#   cp config/opponents/TEMPLATE.env config/opponents/<their-slug>.env
#   scripts/play.sh <their-slug> https://their-tunnel/mcp --role police
#
# Everything here is a per-opponent negotiated value. NEVER edit
# config/<role>/game.json to satisfy one opponent - it rides into the next
# team's handshake. If their constitution differs from ours, make a per-opponent
# config directory instead (see the note at the bottom).
#
# Send them docs/INTEROP_GUIDE.md first; these lines are its section 13 answers.

# -- wire contract (their question 2/3) --------------------------------------
# native    = our four-phase set (handshake / receive_commit / receive_reveal /
#             audit_exchange), request-response
# reference = negotiate / receive_turn / submit_audit / receive_control, push
# If unsure, probe them and trust the probe:
#   scripts/play.sh is not needed for this - run the smoke command directly:
#   PYTHONPATH=src .venv/bin/p2p-pursuit smoke https://their-tunnel/mcp
P2P_DIALECT=native

# Do roles alternate? natural role on sub-games 1/3/5, opposite on 2/4/6.
P2P_ALTERNATE_ROLES=false

# Do they re-negotiate before every sub-game, or handshake once per series?
P2P_HANDSHAKE_PER_SUB_GAME=false

# -- enclosure (book 3.4) -----------------------------------------------------
# EXACTLY ONE side may report a trapped thief, or the series desynchronises.
# true  = our cop claims it     (only if their thief does NOT announce it)
# false = their thief announces it, or the rule is off
P2P_CLAIM_ENCLOSURE=false

# -- capture claim ------------------------------------------------------------
# true if THEIR contract makes the cop's claim protocol-level and unsuppressable
# every turn. Under such a contract our default gating silently forfeits
# captures we earned, so this is worth asking about explicitly.
P2P_ALWAYS_CLAIM=false

# -- scent physics ------------------------------------------------------------
# Three registered models. The first two are the book's multiplicative physics
# (a trail fades but effectively never dies); the third is the reference
# implementation's, and is a genuinely different world.
#
# book_v1                  = rho 0.1, centre 0.9, 4-digit rounding, served
#                            PRE-emission (freshest cell reads 0.81)
# registered_v3            = one-expression update, no rounding, served
#                            POST-emission (freshest cell reads 0.9)
# subtractive_chebyshev_v1 = flat Chebyshev rings 0.9/0.6/0.3, decay by
#                            SUBTRACTING 0.1, merged by max, rounded to 3.
#                            CORE in the league kit, so kit-built teams default
#                            to it.
#
# THE MODEL AND THE DOCTRINE ARE ONE DECISION IN TWO VARIABLES. Never set one
# without the other. Measured 2026-08-17 on hold-out seeds, league points per
# sub-game against the full sparring pool:
#
#                                 book_v1   subtractive
#     config/doctrine.json         13.19       14.11
#     config/doctrine-subtractive  12.82     * 14.94 *
#
# Two things that table settles, both against expectation:
#   1. Subtractive is GOOD FOR US, not a hazard. Its field is brighter and
#      flatter near the emitter, so our police reads it far better (capture 77%
#      -> 98%); the thief leaks more, but the search bought most of that back
#      (survival 89% -> 92.5%). If a kit-built team offers their CORE physics,
#      TAKE IT - it is our best measured cell, +1.75 pts/sub-game over home.
#   2. Either doctrine loses ~0.4-0.8 pts under the other's physics. That loss
#      is silent: nothing errors, the agent just plays worse.
#
# So: subtractive_chebyshev_v1 -> config/doctrine-subtractive.json
#     book_v1 / registered_v3  -> config/doctrine.json
# For a physics we have never met, run `learn tune` under it BEFORE the
# friendly and point P2P_DOCTRINE at the result.
P2P_SCENT_MODEL=book_v1
# Doctrine tuned for that physics. config/doctrine.json is the committed default.
P2P_DOCTRINE=config/doctrine.json

# -- series consensus (reference family only) ---------------------------------
# ON only if their contract specifies the end-of-series digest exchange. It
# rides on submit_audit, so a peer that does not expect it there reads an audit
# package with no records and files a technical loss over a clean series.
P2P_SERIES_CONSENSUS=false

# -- signed series length -----------------------------------------------------
# If their contract says `--games N` must NOT move the signed num_games, pin it
# here so a short compatibility run still signs the agreed six.
# P2P_SIGNED_NUM_GAMES=6

# -- agreed game_id label -----------------------------------------------------
# Only if you agreed an explicit label (e.g. G013). It is a top-level key of the
# consensus hash, so it must be byte-identical on both sides or a cleanly-played
# series fails at the final digest. MUST change per run - a settled series is
# immutable, so reusing a label hashes a new series under a settled one's name.
# P2P_GAME_ID=G013

# -- EMAIL: the line that decides friendly vs counted -------------------------
# The committed role config ships recipient = rmisegal+uoh26finalgame@gmail.com
# and mode = "send". So for a FRIENDLY you MUST override one of them, or the
# practice match files against the lecturer and may be read as THE counted
# encounter - and the book allows exactly one per pair, sealed once filed.
#
# FRIENDLY - pick one:
P2P_EMAIL_MODE=draft
#   ...or send it to yourself instead:
#   P2P_EMAIL_MODE=send
#   P2P_EMAIL_RECIPIENT=your-own-address@example.com
#
# COUNTED - comment out BOTH lines above so the committed recipient applies,
# and launch with --counted. scripts/play.sh refuses a friendly that would
# reach the lecturer, and asks for typed confirmation on a counted match.

# -- constitution -------------------------------------------------------------
# No override needed if they accept ours (sha256 3835f6a1...). If any of the 14
# signed terms differ - `setting` is the usual one - do NOT set env vars for it:
# make config/opponents/<slug>/{police,thief}/ with their game.json and pass
#   --config-dir config/opponents/<slug>/police
# so the change cannot leak into another team's match.

# -- their endpoint -----------------------------------------------------------
# Passed on the command line instead, because quick-tunnel URLs rotate:
#   scripts/play.sh <slug> https://their-tunnel/mcp
# P2P_OPPONENT_URL=
